From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] FPU: C.EQ/C.LT/C.LE and MAX.S/MIN.S in PS2 order

What it broke:
  The compares and MAX.S/MIN.S used host float comparisons. The runtime's
  own float model (ps2_runtime_macros.h) has no Inf or NaN: exponent 255
  is an ordinary exponent and x/0 or an overflow leaves +-0x7FFFFFFF,
  which the host reads as NaN, so every host comparison with it is false
  (C.LT 1.0, 0x7FFFFFFF cleared C; MAX.S 1.0, 0x7FFFFFFF gave 1.0). An
  exponent-0 value with a mantissa compared as a tiny number instead of
  zero (PCSX2 fpuDouble and the model both read it as zero). std::max and
  std::min also gave the first operand for +0 against -0, where PCSX2's
  fp_max/fp_min give +0 and -0. Protector of Earth has 1,205 C.LT.S,
  851 C.EQ.S, 456 C.LE.S, 24 MAX.S and 14 MIN.S.

The changes:
  - ps2_fcompare_key: exponent 0 gives 0, otherwise the bits' magnitude
    with the sign; FPU_C_EQ_S/SEQ, C_OLT_S/LT and C_OLE_S/LE compare the
    keys (the EE's C.LT and C.LE are the codes PS2Recomp names OLT and
    OLE). The unordered forms, which the EE does not have, are unchanged.
  - ps2_fmax/ps2_fmin: PCSX2's fp_max/fp_min on the bits (signed integer
    order, reversed when both are negative); the translator emits them for
    MAX.S/MIN.S instead of std::max/std::min.

How it was checked:
  New per-instruction cases (findings/14 s.8): C.LT with 0x7FFFFFFF and
  with -0x7FFFFFFF, C.EQ of a denormal and zero, C.EQ of -0 and +0,
  MAX.S/MIN.S with 0x7FFFFFFF, two negatives and +0/-0 (expected values
  from PCSX2 FPU.cpp, where it and the runtime's float model agree): they
  failed before and pass after; the earlier compare cases still pass.
  ps2xTest 454/454. The game's code regenerated: only its 24 MAX.S and 14
  MIN.S lines changed; it builds and reaches the first level.

